"""Render a live satellite view of Earth as the GNOME desktop background.

Downloads the latest global true-colour mosaic (NASA GIBS, VIIRS/SNPP),
fills coverage gaps with Blue Marble, and renders one xplanet frame per
distinct logical monitor size.  Frames are published atomically under

    ~/.cache/xplanet-wall/frames/<WIDTH>x<HEIGHT>/<epoch>.png

The companion GNOME Shell extension picks up the newest frame per monitor
and applies it with Meta.Background.set_file().

A live satellite layer draws the ISS (or any NORAD IDs listed in the
config) on the globe: the current sub-satellite marker with height and
speed, a ground track spanning one orbit, and the horizon footprint.
Orbital positions come from the SGP4 propagator built into xplanet, using
TLEs fetched from Celestrak and cached for a few hours.

Everything is cached: the mosaic once per available date, the resized
night map once per mosaic size, and the TLEs per catalogue number.
Re-running is cheap and idempotent.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime as dt
import json
import math
import os
import shutil
import subprocess
